$+\,C),\,\,$where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
  ":question/worked-solution": "First, we separate the variables:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{\text{d}y}{\text{d}x} & =2x\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\,\frac{\text{d}y}{\text{d}x} & =2x\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$

$$
\begin{aligned}\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\,\text{d}y & =\int 2x\,\text{d}x \\ \operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & ={x}^{2}+C\end{aligned}
$$

This is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $y=y(x)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $y.$ This gives

$$
y=\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C)
$$

where $C$ is an arbitrary constant."
